    slug: 'rsi-explained-overbought-oversold',
    title: 'RSI Explained: What Overbought and Oversold Actually Mean',
    description: 'How RSI is calculated, why 70/30 are the conventional thresholds, and the specific way it can mislead you in a strong trend.',
    date: '2026-09-10',
    readTime: '5 min read',
    relatedPath: '/crypto',
    relatedLabel: 'See live RSI on any coin\'s timeframe signals',
    updated: '2026-10-02',
    category: 'Markets',
    keywords: ['rsi indicator', 'rsi indicator meaning', 'rsi indicator buy and sell signals', 'rsi indicator strategy'],
    faqs: [
      { q: 'What does RSI above 70 mean?', a: "Conventionally labeled 'overbought' — the recent price move has been unusually one-sided to the upside over the lookback window. It's a momentum reading, not an automatic sell signal." },
      { q: 'What does RSI below 30 mean?', a: "Conventionally labeled 'oversold' — recent losses have dominated over the lookback window. Like overbought, it describes recent momentum, not a guaranteed reversal point." },
      { q: 'How is RSI calculated?', a: 'RSI compares the average size of up-moves to the average size of down-moves over a lookback period (commonly 14 candles), scaled to a 0-100 number.' },
      { q: 'Can RSI stay overbought for a long time?', a: 'Yes — in a strong sustained uptrend, RSI can sit above 70 for an extended stretch while price keeps climbing. Treating that as an automatic sell signal is a common mistake.' },
      { q: 'What is a good RSI trading strategy?', a: "RSI alone is rarely used as a sole signal — it's more reliable combined with trend/moving-average context and raw momentum, which is why MetalCalc's crypto timeframe signals blend all three into one verdict." },
    ],
    sections: [
      { p: "If you've looked at a stock or crypto chart on any trading app, you've probably seen a wavy line underneath the main price chart labeled \"RSI,\" bouncing between 0 and 100, and wondered what it's actually telling you. RSI stands for Relative Strength Index, and despite the slightly technical-sounding name, the idea behind it is fairly intuitive: it measures how fast and how strongly a price has been rising versus falling recently, and compresses that into a single number between 0 and 100. It's one of the most widely used momentum indicators in trading — and, unfortunately, also one of the most commonly misread." },
      { h2: 'How RSI is actually calculated, in plain terms' },
      { p: "Over a chosen lookback period — most commonly the last 14 candles (a \"candle\" just being one time-interval's worth of price data, whether that's 14 hours, 14 days, or whatever timeframe you're viewing) — RSI compares the average size of the price's up-moves to the average size of its down-moves over that window. If gains have clearly dominated during that period, RSI trends up toward 100. If losses have dominated instead, it trends down toward 0. A reading sitting right around 50 simply means gains and losses have been roughly balanced over that stretch — neither side has been winning convincingly." },
      { h2: 'Why 70 and 30 specifically became the conventional lines' },
      { p: "You'll very commonly see a reading above 70 labeled \"overbought,\" and a reading below 30 labeled \"oversold.\" What these labels actually mean is that the recent price move has been unusually one-sided in that direction over the lookback window — they do not mean the asset is objectively expensive or cheap in any absolute sense, despite how the word \"overbought\" might sound. It's worth knowing that 70 and 30 are simply a widely adopted convention, not some fixed law of how markets must behave — some traders use 80/20 instead for especially volatile assets, specifically because the standard 70/30 thresholds get triggered so often on volatile assets that they stop being a useful signal." },
      { h2: 'The trap almost every beginner falls into: RSI during a strong trend' },
      { p: "Here's the mistake that catches a lot of newer traders off guard: in a genuinely strong, sustained uptrend, RSI can sit above 70 for a very long stretch of time while the price simply keeps on climbing regardless. Treating an overbought RSI reading as an automatic signal to sell has caused countless traders to exit a winning position far too early, only to watch the price keep rising without them. The underlying reason this happens is that RSI describes recent momentum — it is not a guaranteed signal that a reversal is about to happen. Strong trends can and do stay \"overbought\" or \"oversold\" by RSI's standards for extended periods." },
      { h2: 'A simple way to avoid the trap' },
      { p: "Rather than treating an overbought or oversold reading as an instant buy/sell trigger on its own, it helps to check it against the broader trend first — is the price in a strong uptrend, a strong downtrend, or genuinely going sideways? An overbought reading inside a strong uptrend is a very different situation from an overbought reading in a market that's already gone sideways for a while. Combining RSI with some sense of the overall trend direction, rather than reacting to RSI alone, is a much more reliable habit." },
      { h2: 'How MetalCalc actually uses RSI' },
      { p: "MetalCalc's Crypto timeframe signals combine RSI with a moving-average crossover (a way of tracking whether a shorter-term average price has crossed above or below a longer-term average, which helps capture the trend direction) and raw price momentum, blending all three into one composite verdict per timeframe, from 15 minutes out to 3 months. RSI is never used alone to decide the final signal — precisely because of the strong-trend trap described above." },
    ],
